<p>The UK Parliament has invited Meta, Google, OpenAI and Anthropic to appear before the Business, Innovation, Science and Trade Committee on October 13, 2026, for an evidence session focused on AI security. The committee says the session will support its work on the UK's economic strategy for AI. The important part for AI teams is the list of questions: independent testing, serious-incident reporting and the risk of a safety race between companies and countries.</p>
<figure class="article-image"><img src="/assets/images/blog/uk-ai-security-hearing-october-2026.svg" width="1200" height="630" alt="UK Parliament AI security hearing with questions about independent testing and incident reporting"></figure>
<h2>What the UK Parliament confirmed</h2><p>The committee published letters on September 22 inviting Meta, Google, OpenAI and Anthropic to give evidence on Tuesday, October 13. The UK Government's AI Security Institute has also been invited.</p><p>The committee has not announced the companies' answers because the session has not happened yet. That distinction matters: the published material is a request for evidence, not a new AI regulation or a finding against any company.</p>
<h2>The three questions that matter most</h2><ol><li><strong>Who decides when independent testing is needed?</strong> The committee asks whether AI companies should decide when their models are powerful enough to require independent safety testing, or whether testing should become legally mandatory.</li><li><strong>Should serious incidents be reported?</strong> The questions include deceptive behaviour, bypassed safeguards, unauthorised replication and signs that human control may be failing.</li><li><strong>How do you avoid a race to the bottom?</strong> The committee asks what common international requirements should apply if competition between companies or countries creates pressure to reduce safety work.</li></ol>
<h2>Why independent testing is becoming a bigger issue</h2><p>Internal evaluations are useful because model developers have access to training details, safety systems and deployment context. But an outside evaluator can bring a different incentive structure and challenge assumptions the developer may not notice.</p><p>Anthropic's recent <a href="/claude/anthropic-accenture-embedded-ai-evaluation-2026">embedded evaluation partnership with Accenture</a> shows one way the industry is trying to add external scrutiny closer to model development. The UK hearing could push the same question into public policy: which evaluations should remain voluntary and which should be required?</p>
<h2>What incident reporting could change</h2><p>Serious-incident reporting would create a clearer record of failures across vendors. For enterprise buyers, that could make vendor-risk reviews easier because incident history would become a more useful input than marketing claims alone.</p><p>But reporting rules also need definitions. A minor hallucination, a successful prompt injection and a model taking an unauthorised real-world action are not the same event. Any future requirement would need clear thresholds, timelines and evidence standards.</p>
<h2>What AI companies should prepare now</h2><table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Useful preparation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Evaluation</td><td>Keep repeatable test sets and document major capability changes.</td></tr><tr><td>Incidents</td><td>Maintain an internal severity scale, evidence trail and response owner.</td></tr><tr><td>Agent safety</td><td>Log tool calls, approvals, credentials and external side effects.</td></tr><tr><td>Independent review</td><td>Define which tests should be reproducible by an outside evaluator.</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h2>What this means for AI buyers and developers</h2><p>Businesses should ask vendors whether important safety claims have been independently tested, how incidents are classified and whether the vendor can provide evidence after a major model update. Developers should also avoid treating a model card as the complete security review for an agent that can access production systems.</p><p>The recent <a href="/tools-guide/plugin4shell-ai-coding-agent-security-2026">Plugin4Shell disclosure</a> is a useful reminder that the surrounding software and supply chain can create risk even when the underlying model is not the vulnerable component.</p>
<h2>What is not decided yet</h2><p>The October session does not itself create mandatory independent testing or mandatory incident reporting. It is an evidence-gathering step. The committee says further details will be published later.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>When is the UK AI security hearing?</h3><p>The Business, Innovation, Science and Trade Committee invited the companies to an evidence session on October 13, 2026.</p><h3>Which companies were invited?</h3><p>Meta, Google, OpenAI and Anthropic were invited. The UK Government's AI Security Institute was also invited.</p><h3>Does the hearing create a new AI law?</h3><p>No. The published notice is an invitation to give evidence and a set of questions for the committee's work.</p>
